# Parsed by Cloudflare Workers static assets (and by Netlify — the syntax is shared and
# every rule below behaves identically on both platforms, so this one file serves both
# hosts during the migration). First match wins, top to bottom.
#
# ⚠️ KEEP EVERY STATIC RULE (no * and no :placeholder) ABOVE THE FIRST DYNAMIC ONE. Workers
# looks static rules up by exact path before it tries any dynamic rule, but only the ones
# above the first * or : line; anything below it counts as dynamic, and there may be no
# more than 100 dynamic rules (2,000 static). Ordered this way, the file means the same
# thing on both hosts. scripts/redirects.test.mjs reads this file the way the host does and
# fails the gate when it and the app's router (portal/01-core.jsx) disagree.

# Workers runs with html_handling "none" (see wrangler.jsonc), so the implicit
# / -> index.html mapping other hosts do for free must be explicit here.
/            /index.html    200

# Netlify 301-normalizes trailing slashes BEFORE _redirects runs (probed 2026-08-05:
# /portal/ -> 301 /portal, /admin/ -> 301 /admin), so the trailing-slash 301 rules are inert
# on Netlify and exist to make Workers behave byte-identically instead of depending on
# html_handling slash rules.
/admin       /admin.html    200
/admin/      /admin         301

/my-quotes   /my-quotes.html  200
/my-quotes/  /my-quotes       301

# ── THE APP'S ADDRESSES: /<page>[/<sub>], NO /portal (Carolyn 2026-10-12) ─────────────────
# "It's app.structurestudiosuite.com forward slash advance. Like, so let's remove portal from
# anywhere." Every page of the app is portal.html, rewritten — 200, never 301, so the address
# the user typed is the address they keep and the app reads the path on boot.
#
# ONE PAIR OF RULES PER PAGE, ON PURPOSE: `/<slug>` here and `/<slug>/*` in the dynamic block
# below. A root catch-all (`/* /portal.html 200`) would swallow the public designer at / and
# /?client=…, /my-quotes, /admin and every asset (the vendored libraries, the compiled
# bundles, sw.js, the manifest, the icons). Every page the router knows (TAB_META in
# portal/01-core.jsx, and the old names it still reads) needs its pair here and in _headers;
# scripts/redirects.test.mjs fails when one is missing or a rule names a slug the router
# does not know.
#
# /login is the bare app address (the old bare /portal): the sign-in form, or for someone
# signed in their landing page. /console is the Admin page, because /admin stays admin.html.
/login                         /portal.html   200
/designer                      /portal.html   200
/advanced                      /portal.html   200
/contacts                      /portal.html   200
/conversations                 /portal.html   200
/inventory                     /portal.html   200
/orders                        /portal.html   200
/build-schedule                /portal.html   200
/delivery-schedule             /portal.html   200
/repairs                       /portal.html   200
/commissions                   /portal.html   200
/calls                         /portal.html   200
/rent-to-own-contracts         /portal.html   200
/reports                       /portal.html   200
/self-serve-display-units      /portal.html   200
/support                       /portal.html   200
/settings                      /portal.html   200
/quickbooks                    /portal.html   200
/on-demand-pricing             /portal.html   200
/view-3d                       /portal.html   200
/accounts                      /portal.html   200
/console                       /portal.html   200
/projects                      /portal.html   200
# Old page names the router still reads and rewrites in the address bar (SS_TAB_ALIASES and
# ssCanonRoute): Leads became Contacts, Releases became Support, and the Pipeline page and
# the Leads tab's boards are views inside the Contacts hub now.
/leads                         /portal.html   200
